WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.870
Chaining needs extra structure

2
00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:06.830
a list for every slot.

3
00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:08.550
There is another way

4
00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:10.820
with nothing but the table itself.

5
00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:13.820
Every entry lives in the table array

6
00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:15.060
in a slot.

7
00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:17.680
If the slot you want is taken

8
00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.520
you do not build a chain.

9
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.610
You look for another slot.

10
00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:24.408
This is called open addressing

11
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.287
because the address of an entry is

12
00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:28.310
not fixed by its hash alone.

13
00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:30.903
In this video we'll see three ways

14
00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:32.510
to choose the next slot

15
00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:35.510
what happens to the cost as the table fills

16
00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:38.660
and why deleting is the tricky part.

17
00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.110
The simplest rule is linear probing.

18
00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:44.170
Try the slot the hash gives you.

19
00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:46.430
If it is taken, try the next one

20
00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:49.280
then the next, wrapping around at the end.

21
00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:52.540
Thirteen slots, and the keys eighteen

22
00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:53.920
forty-one, twenty-two.

23
00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:57.020
Their remainders are five, two and nine

24
00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:58.750
and all three slots are free.

25
00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:01.870
Forty-four also wants slot five.

26
00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:03.710
It is taken by eighteen

27
00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:05.810
so it goes to slot six.

28
00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:06.980
Two probes.

29
00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:09.700
Fifty-nine goes to slot seven.

30
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.960
Thirty-two wants slot six, finds forty-four

31
00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:17.380
finds fifty-nine, and settles in slot eight.

32
00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:18.810
Three probes.

33
00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:21.384
Thirty-one wants slot five

34
00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:23.090
and finds it taken.

35
00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:27.690
Six, seven, eight, nine, all taken.

36
00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:31.640
It lands in slot ten, after six probes.

37
00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:34.540
And seventy-three wants slot eight

38
00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:36.240
and ends in slot eleven.

39
00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:37.716
Look at the table now

40
00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:40.859
slots five to eleven form one unbroken

41
00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:42.550
run of seven entries.

42
00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:45.380
That run is called a cluster

43
00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:48.130
and it is the weakness of linear probing.

44
00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:50.250
The bigger a cluster is

45
00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:52.710
the more likely the next key falls into it

46
00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:54.330
and the bigger it gets.

47
00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:56.440
Clusters feed themselves.

48
00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:59.200
Nineteen probes for eight keys.

49
00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:02.430
Two better rules spread the probes out.

50
00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:06.080
Quadratic probing jumps by growing steps

51
00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.560
the first slot, then one further

52
00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:11.810
then four further, then nine, and so on.

53
00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:16.070
Thirty-one now probes five, six, nine

54
00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:18.650
and one, and stops in slot one

55
00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:20.090
far from the others.

56
00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:22.890
Seventy-three finds its own slot, eight

57
00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:23.600
free.

58
00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:27.080
Fourteen probes in all, instead of nineteen.

59
00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:30.400
The price is a guarantee that is weaker

60
00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.727
with a prime table size

61
00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:34.641
quadratic probing only promises

62
00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:36.586
to find a free slot while the table

63
00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:38.430
is less than half full.

64
00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:41.582
Double hashing uses a second hash

65
00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:43.804
function to decide the step.

66
00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:45.664
Different keys that start at the

67
00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:47.540
same slot take different steps

68
00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:49.610
so they separate at once.

69
00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:52.618
Here the step is eleven minus

70
00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:54.870
the key modulo eleven.

71
00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:56.690
Forty-four takes a step of eleven

72
00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:58.630
and lands in slot three.

73
00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:03.750
Thirty-one takes steps of two: five, seven

74
00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:05.640
nine, eleven.

75
00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:07.760
Twelve probes in all.

76
00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:09.760
With a prime table size

77
00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:12.056
every slot is eventually reachable

78
00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:14.464
because no step shares a factor

79
00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:16.110
with the table size.

80
00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:19.180
How much does a lookup cost?

81
00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:22.220
That depends on one number: the load factor

82
00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:24.370
the fraction of slots in use.

83
00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.155
For linear probing

84
00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:28.616
a successful search needs about a half times

85
00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:31.286
one plus one over one minus alpha probes

86
00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:33.929
and an unsuccessful one needs about a half

87
00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:36.480
times one plus the square of that.

88
00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:38.359
We measured it on a table

89
00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:40.380
of ten thousand slots.

90
00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:42.067
At a load of one half

91
00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:44.415
one point five probes to find a key

92
00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:46.660
and two point four to miss one.

93
00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:49.160
At three quarters, two and a half

94
00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:50.390
and eight and a half.

95
00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:52.150
At nine tenths

96
00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:54.310
a hit costs five and a half probes

97
00:03:54.310 --> 00:03:57.270
but a miss costs about fifty.

98
00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:59.901
The cost explodes as the table fills

99
00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:01.602
so open addressing keeps

100
00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:03.140
its load factor low.

101
00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:05.050
Now deleting.

102
00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:07.850
The obvious way is to empty the slot.

103
00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:09.110
Let's try it

104
00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:11.970
remove forty-four from the linear table

105
00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:13.200
slot six.

106
00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:15.840
Then look for thirty-two.

107
00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:17.780
Its hash says slot six.

108
00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:19.640
Slot six is empty.

109
00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:22.280
A lookup stops at the first empty slot

110
00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:24.260
so it reports: missing.

111
00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:27.070
But thirty-two is in slot eight.

112
00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:29.590
By emptying slot six

113
00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:32.290
we cut the probe sequence that thirty-two

114
00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:33.810
and thirty-one, and seventy-three

115
00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:34.670
depend on.

116
00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:36.550
They are still stored

117
00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:38.300
but nobody can reach them.

118
00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:42.300
The fix is a tombstone: a marker that means

119
00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:44.850
something was here, keep going.

120
00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:48.050
With a tombstone in slot six

121
00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:50.630
looking for thirty-two probes six, seven

122
00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:52.000
eight, and finds it.

123
00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:54.530
Tombstones need three rules.

124
00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:57.630
A lookup skips them and keeps probing.

125
00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:00.270
An insert may reuse a tombstone

126
00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:02.223
but only after the probe has

127
00:05:02.290 --> 00:05:03.476
reached an empty slot

128
00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:04.761
to be sure the key is not

129
00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:06.580
stored further along.

130
00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:10.140
And tombstones count toward the load factor

131
00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:13.380
so a table full of tombstones is rebuilt

132
00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:15.050
next time we'll see how.

133
00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:16.930
To sum up.

134
00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:19.655
Open addressing keeps every entry in the

135
00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:22.560
array and probes when a slot is taken

136
00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:25.910
linear, quadratic or double hashing.

137
00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:28.870
Keep the table less than about half full

138
00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:31.140
and delete with tombstones.

139
00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:32.520
Next

140
00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:34.650
what to do when the table does fill up.

141
00:05:35.277 --> 00:05:37.837
Now open addressing in Python.

142
00:05:38.417 --> 00:05:40.727
Everything depends on one function

143
00:05:40.727 --> 00:05:43.367
the slot of the i-th probe.

144
00:05:43.367 --> 00:05:45.027
Compress the hash code first

145
00:05:45.027 --> 00:05:48.337
and then add an offset that depends on i.

146
00:05:48.687 --> 00:05:51.177
Linear adds i.

147
00:05:51.177 --> 00:05:53.504
Quadratic adds i squared.

148
00:05:53.597 --> 00:05:56.608
Double hashing adds i times a step

149
00:05:56.697 --> 00:05:58.137
that depends on the key

150
00:05:58.137 --> 00:06:00.637
the largest prime below the table size

151
00:06:00.637 --> 00:06:03.497
minus the hash code modulo that prime

152
00:06:03.497 --> 00:06:05.367
which is never zero.

153
00:06:05.717 --> 00:06:07.864
The table size is a prime

154
00:06:07.947 --> 00:06:09.361
so that the double hashing step

155
00:06:09.407 --> 00:06:11.477
always reaches every slot.

156
00:06:12.057 --> 00:06:14.316
Search walks the probe sequence.

157
00:06:14.387 --> 00:06:16.412
An empty slot means the key

158
00:06:16.487 --> 00:06:19.017
is not in the table, and we stop.

159
00:06:19.367 --> 00:06:21.877
A tombstone is a special marker object.

160
00:06:21.877 --> 00:06:24.597
When we see one, we remember the first one

161
00:06:24.597 --> 00:06:25.817
and keep going

162
00:06:25.817 --> 00:06:27.887
because the key may be further along.

163
00:06:28.237 --> 00:06:30.656
Only a slot with an equal key ends

164
00:06:30.727 --> 00:06:32.747
the search with success.

165
00:06:32.747 --> 00:06:34.384
Put uses the same search

166
00:06:34.447 --> 00:06:35.273
and then writes into the

167
00:06:35.307 --> 00:06:36.629
first tombstone it saw

168
00:06:36.687 --> 00:06:38.290
or into the empty slot where

169
00:06:38.347 --> 00:06:39.437
the search stopped.

170
00:06:40.017 --> 00:06:42.927
Run the eight keys through all three rules.

171
00:06:42.927 --> 00:06:45.107
The trace is printed for every put

172
00:06:45.107 --> 00:06:48.617
the hash, the index, the slots probed.

173
00:06:48.967 --> 00:06:50.743
Linear probing ends with

174
00:06:50.817 --> 00:06:52.717
a run of seven entries

175
00:06:52.717 --> 00:06:55.139
from slot five to slot eleven.

176
00:06:55.217 --> 00:06:57.278
Quadratic and double hashing spread

177
00:06:57.337 --> 00:06:59.327
the same keys over the table.

178
00:06:59.677 --> 00:07:01.087
Counting probes

179
00:07:01.087 --> 00:07:03.167
nineteen for linear probing

180
00:07:03.167 --> 00:07:05.507
fourteen for quadratic

181
00:07:05.507 --> 00:07:07.117
twelve for double hashing.

182
00:07:07.697 --> 00:07:09.000
And the delete.

183
00:07:09.087 --> 00:07:11.035
The class takes a flag that

184
00:07:11.107 --> 00:07:12.667
turns tombstones off

185
00:07:12.667 --> 00:07:15.177
only so that we can show the mistake.

186
00:07:15.527 --> 00:07:17.297
With a tombstone

187
00:07:17.297 --> 00:07:20.417
looking for thirty-two probes six, seven

188
00:07:20.417 --> 00:07:21.937
eight, and finds it.

189
00:07:21.937 --> 00:07:23.997
With a plain empty slot

190
00:07:23.997 --> 00:07:27.327
the lookup stops at six and says missing.

191
00:07:27.677 --> 00:07:29.147
A test checks both

192
00:07:29.147 --> 00:07:32.037
the naive delete really does lose the key.

193
00:07:32.617 --> 00:07:34.447
Finally, the measurement.

194
00:07:34.447 --> 00:07:35.947
Ten thousand slots

195
00:07:35.947 --> 00:07:37.964
the same random keys every run

196
00:07:38.027 --> 00:07:39.917
and we count the slots examined

197
00:07:39.967 --> 00:07:41.497
by every search.

198
00:07:41.847 --> 00:07:43.297
The measured numbers sit

199
00:07:43.357 --> 00:07:45.057
right on the formulas

200
00:07:45.057 --> 00:07:46.857
up to a load of nine tenths

201
00:07:46.857 --> 00:07:49.727
where a miss costs fifty-one probes.

202
00:07:50.307 --> 00:07:51.872
In the standard library

203
00:07:51.937 --> 00:07:53.690
Python's own dictionary is

204
00:07:53.757 --> 00:07:55.117
an open addressing table.

205
00:07:55.117 --> 00:07:56.681
It does not probe linearly

206
00:07:56.737 --> 00:07:59.150
it mixes the higher bits of the hash

207
00:07:59.217 --> 00:08:00.737
code into the probe sequence

208
00:08:00.737 --> 00:08:02.747
to avoid clusters.

209
00:08:03.097 --> 00:08:04.671
And it marks deleted entries

210
00:08:04.727 --> 00:08:07.287
with a dummy marker, a tombstone

211
00:08:07.287 --> 00:08:08.377
just like ours.

212
00:08:08.957 --> 00:08:10.587
Mistakes to avoid.

213
00:08:10.587 --> 00:08:13.087
A table size that is not prime

214
00:08:13.087 --> 00:08:14.500
with double hashing

215
00:08:14.567 --> 00:08:16.033
the step may share a factor

216
00:08:16.087 --> 00:08:17.135
with the table size

217
00:08:17.187 --> 00:08:18.618
and the probe sequence visits

218
00:08:18.667 --> 00:08:20.837
only part of the table.

219
00:08:21.187 --> 00:08:22.949
Treating a tombstone as an

220
00:08:23.017 --> 00:08:24.320
empty slot in a search.

221
00:08:24.377 --> 00:08:27.431
And inserting into the first tombstone without

222
00:08:27.497 --> 00:08:29.717
checking the rest of the probe sequence

223
00:08:29.717 --> 00:08:32.667
you may create a duplicate key.

224
00:08:33.017 --> 00:08:34.668
The tests cover both.

225
00:08:34.747 --> 00:08:37.389
Putting a key again after deleting

226
00:08:37.467 --> 00:08:39.630
an entry earlier in its probe sequence

227
00:08:39.687 --> 00:08:43.857
must update the key, not add a second copy.

228
00:08:44.437 --> 00:08:46.727
To recap: slot function

229
00:08:46.727 --> 00:08:48.767
search that skips tombstones

230
00:08:48.767 --> 00:08:51.487
and a load factor that stays low.

231
00:08:51.487 --> 00:08:54.477
Next: what happens when the table fills.

